+\reply
+We have clarified how \tool{} differs from and complements SmartFuzz in Section~VI, and we have extended the comparison in Section~IV. SmartFuzz uses LLMs to synthesize new seeds from features extracted from official DBMS documentation and historical bug-triggering inputs, whereas \tool{} transfers existing test cases from mature DBMSs and preserves their cross-statement structures. The two approaches therefore draw on different seed sources.
+
+To compare them under the same setting, we use the seeds released by SmartFuzz and run them with \griffin{} on all four target DBMSs, with the same DBMS versions, resource allocation, campaign duration, and coverage measurement as the other tools in RQ2. \TODO{coverage and bug results of SmartFuzz vs.\ \tool{}}
+
+To examine their complementarity, we further run \griffin{} with the union of the SmartFuzz and \tool{} seeds. \TODO{results of the combined seeds, the branches and bugs found only by each seed source, and the MonetDB case}
+
+We have also acknowledged the limitations of this comparison in Section~IV. SmartFuzz was originally evaluated with its extended \textsc{Squirrel}, while we use \griffin{} for all seed sources to isolate the effect of the seeds; our results therefore reflect seed quality under the same fuzzer and do not represent the performance of the complete SmartFuzz system. \TODO{number of runs per configuration}
