+\reply
+We have added a discussion of SmartFuzz in Section~VI, which clarifies that SmartFuzz synthesizes new seeds from DBMS documentation and historical bug-triggering inputs, whereas \tool{} transfers existing test cases from mature DBMSs. We have also included SmartFuzz as a baseline in RQ2 and examined the complementarity of the two seed sources. Details can be found in \metareviewref{3}.
 
 % Source: Reviewer B, Detailed 3.
 \reviewcomment{2.5} \textbf{Potentially inaccurate characterization of SEDAR.} The paper characterizes SEDAR's comment-and-restore mechanism as ``destructive sanitization'' that ``sacrifices semantic depth.'' However, SEDAR's mechanism is designed to temporarily hide target-specific constructs from downstream fuzzers' strict parsers during mutation and restore them afterward. This is a pragmatic compatibility strategy, not a destructive removal of query logic.
